  "texto": "Three years in, Maya's coworker Jo asked her a question. It was a simple one. 'When was the last time you did something just because you wanted to?' Maya opened her mouth to answer. And couldn't think of one. That silence was the first crack. Not a dramatic escape. Just a question she couldn't answer. It took months. Quiet conversations. A counselor. A plan. Friends she slowly found again. Here's what movies get wrong. Leaving isn't one brave moment. It's a process, and it needs support, because the exit can be the riskiest part. Five moves. Overwhelming attention. Isolation. Rewriting your memory. A debt you can never pay. And the switch between cruelty and kindness. Not one of them leaves a bruise. That's exactly why they work. If you recognized even one of these in your own life, that doesn't make you weak. It means it worked on you the way it's designed to work on anyone. Talk to someone you trust, or reach out to a professional or a local helpline. You don't have to sort this out alone. Now I want to ask you something. Which move would you have caught first? Comment the number, one to five. No judgment. That's the whole point. Next video: the apology that isn't one.",
